                    <div>
                        <strong class="d-block">Understanding the Stored Procedure + ADO.NET + jQuery AJAX Pipeline</strong>
                        Here is the complete architectural guide explaining how your <code>Customers Stored Procedure.sql</code> connects to the application via <code>Web.config</code> and feeds into the interactive grids.
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Step 1: Web.config Connection String -->
                <div class="workflow-step-card">
                    <div class="workflow-step-header">
                        <div class="d-flex align-items-center">
                            <span class="workflow-badge me-3">Step 1</span>
                            <h5 class="fw-bold mb-0">Connection String in Web.config &amp; Stored Procedure Definition</h5>
                        </div>
                        <span class="text-muted small"><i class="fa-solid fa-gear me-1"></i> Database Configuration Layer</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="card-body p-4">
                        <p class="text-muted">
                            The connection string is registered in <code>Web.config</code> under the <code>&lt;connectionStrings&gt;</code> section with the key <code>DBConn</code>.
                            The SQL script creates the table and the stored procedure <code>dbo.sp_SearchCustomers</code>.
                        </p>
                        <div class="row">
                            <div class="col-lg-6">
                                <h6 class="fw-bold text-dark"><i class="fa-solid fa-code text-primary me-1"></i> Web.config Configuration</h6>
                                <pre class="code-block"><code>&lt;configuration&gt;
  &lt;connectionStrings&gt;
    &lt;add name="DBConn" 
         connectionString="Data Source=.;Initial Catalog=Assignmet;User ID=sa;Password=mcc#1234" 
         providerName="System.Data.SqlClient" /&gt;
  &lt;/connectionStrings&gt;
&lt;/configuration&gt;</code></pre>
                            </div>
                            <div class="col-lg-6">
                                <h6 class="fw-bold text-dark"><i class="fa-solid fa-database text-warning me-1"></i> Stored Procedure (dbo.sp_SearchCustomers)</h6>
                                <pre class="code-block"><code>CREATE OR ALTER PROCEDURE dbo.sp_SearchCustomers
    @@SearchTerm NVARCHAR(50) = NULL
AS
BEGIN
    SET NOCOUNT ON;
    SELECT CustomerID, ContactName, City, Country, Email, Phone
    FROM dbo.Customers
    WHERE (@@SearchTerm IS NULL OR @@SearchTerm = '')
       OR ContactName LIKE '%' + @@SearchTerm + '%'
       OR City LIKE '%' + @@SearchTerm + '%'
       OR Country LIKE '%' + @@SearchTerm + '%'
    ORDER BY CustomerID ASC;
END;</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Step 2: ADO.NET Stored Procedure Execution -->
                <div class="workflow-step-card">
                    <div class="workflow-step-header">
                        <div class="d-flex align-items-center">
                            <span class="workflow-badge me-3">Step 2</span>
                            <h5 class="fw-bold mb-0">Executing Stored Procedure via ADO.NET in C#</h5>
                        </div>
                        <span class="text-muted small"><i class="fa-solid fa-server me-1"></i> Data Access Layer</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="card-body p-4">
                        <p class="text-muted">
                            Instead of writing inline SQL queries, the application retrieves <code>DBConn</code> from <code>ConfigurationManager</code> and invokes <code>dbo.sp_SearchCustomers</code> using <code>SqlCommand</code> with <code>CommandType.StoredProcedure</code>:
                        </p>

                        <div class="row">
                            <div class="col-lg-7">
                                <h6 class="fw-bold text-dark"><i class="fa-solid fa-code text-primary me-1"></i> ADO.NET Stored Procedure Invocation</h6>
                                <pre class="code-block"><code>string constr = ConfigurationManager.ConnectionStrings["DBConn"].ConnectionString;
List&lt;Customer&gt; customers = new List&lt;Customer&gt;();

using (SqlConnection conn = new SqlConnection(constr))
{
    using (SqlCommand cmd = new SqlCommand("dbo.sp_SearchCustomers", conn))
    {
        cmd.CommandType = CommandType.StoredProcedure;
        
        // Pass optional filter parameter
        if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(searchTerm))
            cmd.Parameters.Add("@SearchTerm", SqlDbType.NVarChar, 50).Value = searchTerm;
        else
            cmd.Parameters.Add("@SearchTerm", SqlDbType.NVarChar, 50).Value = DBNull.Value;

        conn.Open();
        using (SqlDataReader reader = cmd.ExecuteReader())
        {
            while (reader.Read())
            {
                customers.Add(new Customer {
                    CustomerID = Convert.ToInt32(reader["CustomerID"]),
                    ContactName = reader["ContactName"].ToString(),
                    City = reader["City"].ToString(),
                    Country = reader["Country"].ToString()
                });
            }
        }
    }
}</code></pre>
                            </div>
                            <div class="col-lg-5">
                                <h6 class="fw-bold text-dark">Why Stored Procedures?</h6>
                                <div class="bg-light p-3 rounded-2 border small">
                                    <ul class="list-unstyled mb-0">
                                        <li class="mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-shield-halved text-success me-2"></i><strong>SQL Injection Prevention:</strong> Parameterized stored procedures prevent malicious input execution.</li>
                                        <li class="mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-gauge-high text-primary me-2"></i><strong>Pre-compiled Performance:</strong> SQL Server caches execution plans for stored procedures, minimizing overhead.</li>
                                        <li class="mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-network-wired text-info me-2"></i><strong>Reduced Network Traffic:</strong> Only the procedure name and parameters are transmitted over the wire.</li>
                                        <li><i class="fa-solid fa-arrows-split-up-and-left text-warning me-2"></i><strong>Clean Separation of Concerns:</strong> Business logic and schema are managed cleanly inside the database tier.</li>
                                    </ul>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Step 3: Controller Action Methods -->
                <div class="workflow-step-card">
                    <div class="workflow-step-header">
                        <div class="d-flex align-items-center">
